WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.754
FedRAMP is built on NIST guidance, particularly NIST Special Publication 800-53.

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:09.935
NIST SP 800-53 provides a catalog of security and privacy controls used across federal

00:00:09.935 --> 00:00:11.895
information systems and programs.

00:00:11.895 --> 00:00:12.470
In FedRAMP,

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:16.580
those controls provide the foundation for many security requirements and assessment

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:17.320
activities.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:21.100
They help define what a cloud service provider needs to implement, document, assess,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.520
and monitor.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.620
Controls cover many areas, including access control, incident response,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.960
configuration management, contingency planning, identification and authentication,

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.280
audit logging, risk assessment, system communications, and vulnerability management.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.100
The NIST relationship is important because FedRAMP is not invented separately from federal

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.299
risk management practices.

00:00:39.299 --> 00:00:43.000
It is connected to the same control language used in broader federal cybersecurity

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:43.775
governance.

00:00:43.775 --> 00:00:47.323
Cloud providers and agencies use these controls to structure security plans,

00:00:47.323 --> 00:00:51.340
implementation statements, assessment procedures, findings, remediation plans,

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.085
and monitoring activities.

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:55.729
Control implementation is not just a paperwork activity.

00:00:55.729 --> 00:01:00.110
A control may involve technical configuration, operational procedures, policy, monitoring,

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.760
evidence generation, and clear ownership.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:03.425
For learners,

00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:07.570
the key takeaway is that FedRAMP translates NIST control expectations into a standardized

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.835
cloud security assessment and monitoring model for federal use.

00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:12.965
This connection also helps technical teams.

00:01:12.965 --> 00:01:16.095
When engineers understand the control families behind FedRAMP requirements,

00:01:16.095 --> 00:01:19.460
they can connect architecture, configuration, logging, identity,

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.493
and operational practices to specific risk objectives.

00:01:22.493 --> 00:01:25.880
The control language provides a bridge between implementation and governance.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.800
It lets a technical safeguard become part of a larger story about how the cloud service

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.880
protects federal information and supports agency risk decisions.
